      "body": "## Summary\nThe `_checkMintCap` function uses `&&` (AND) instead of `||` (OR) in the small market branch, allowing mint amounts up to 2^96 (~7.9×10²⁸) to bypass intended caps when minting more than the market's total supply.\n\n## Finding Description\n\nThe `_checkMintCap` function enforces different restrictions based on market size. For small markets (where `marketBaseTokenSupply <= 2^noCapLimit`), the code attempts to cap mint amounts to prevent destabilization:\n\n```solidity\n// src/lex/latentswap/libraries/LatentSwapLogic.sol:1066-1069\nif (marketBaseTokenSupply <= (1 << noCapLimit)) {\n    if ((mintAmount > FixedPoint.Q96) && (mintAmount > marketBaseTokenSupply)) {\n        revert LSErrors.E_LEX_MintCapExceeded();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**The Flaw:** The condition uses `&&` requiring **both** conditions to be true for a revert:\n1. `mintAmount > Q96` (where Q96 = 2^96 = 79,228,162,514,264,337,593,543,950,336)\n2. `mintAmount > marketBaseTokenSupply`\n\nThis creates a bypass: if either condition is false, the check passes. Since Q96 is astronomically large (10²⁸), practically all real-world mint amounts will be below it, making the first condition false and allowing the entire check to pass regardless of the second condition.\n\n**Attack Scenario:**\n1. Market has `baseSupply = 1 ETH` (considered \"small\" with default `noCapLimit = 60`)\n2. Attacker mints `100 ETH` (100x the market size)\n3. Check evaluates: `(100e18 > Q96) && (100e18 > 1e18)` → `false && true` → `false`\n4. Transaction succeeds, bypassing the intended cap\n\nThe comment states: *\"for small markets we can mint up to 2^96\"* - but the intent was clearly to limit mints to `min(Q96, marketBaseTokenSupply)`, not allow anything below Q96 regardless of market size.\n\n**Code Flow:**\n```solidity\n// LatentSwapLogic.sol:319-325\nfunction _calcMint(\n    LexParams memory lexParams,\n    LexFullState memory marketState,\n    uint256 baseTokenAmountIn\n) internal pure returns (uint256, uint256) {\n    // Check if mint amount is too large given market size\n    _checkMintCap(\n        marketState.baseTokenSupply,\n        marketState.lexState.lastETWAPBaseSupply,\n        baseTokenAmountIn,\n        marketState.lexConfig.noCapLimit\n    );\n    // ... continues with mint calculation\n}\n```\n\nEvery mint operation calls `_calcMint`, which invokes `_checkMintCap`. The flawed logic allows small markets to accept disproportionately large mints that should be rejected.\n\n## Impact Explanation\n\n**HIGH Severity** - This vulnerability completely undermines the mint cap protection mechanism for small markets:\n\n1. **Market Manipulation:** Attackers can flood small markets with liquidity orders of magnitude larger than the market size (e.g., 100x, 1000x)\n2. **ETWAP Bypass:** The Exponential Time-Weighted Average Price (ETWAP) flow control is circumvented, as mints don't respect the gradual growth constraints\n3. **Price Destabilization:** Sudden massive liquidity injections can distort pricing mechanisms, LTV ratios, and synthetic token valuations\n4. **Liquidity Imbalance:** The protocol's balanced mint/redeem flow is broken, allowing asymmetric position building\n5. **Economic Exploitation:** Attackers can mint enormous positions in nascent markets, manipulate prices through other operations, then redeem at profit\n\nThe issue affects all markets during their \"small\" phase (below `2^noCapLimit` threshold), which for 18-decimal tokens is below ~1.15×10¹⁸ tokens - essentially all early-stage markets.\n\n## Likelihood Explanation\n\n**HIGH Likelihood:**\n\n- No special privileges required - any user can exploit this\n- The vulnerability is present in every mint operation on small markets\n- Small markets are common during protocol launch and new market creation\n- The flaw is deterministic and easily reproducible\n- Default `noCapLimit = 60` means markets below ~1.15 billion billion tokens are vulnerable\n- Attackers can intentionally keep markets \"small\" while exploiting the bypass\n\nThe combination of ease of exploitation and wide attack surface (all small markets) makes this highly likely to occur.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nReplace the `&&` (AND) operator with `||` (OR) to enforce both limits:\n\n```solidity\nfunction _checkMintCap(\n    uint256 marketBaseTokenSupply,\n    uint256 eTWAPBaseTokenSupply,\n    uint256 mintAmount,\n    uint8 noCapLimit\n) internal pure {\n    unchecked {\n        if (marketBaseTokenSupply <= (1 << noCapLimit)) {\n            // FIX: Use OR instead of AND to enforce BOTH limits\n            if ((mintAmount > FixedPoint.Q96) || (mintAmount > marketBaseTokenSupply)) {\n                revert LSErrors.E_LEX_MintCapExceeded();\n            }\n        } else if (\n            (mintAmount > marketBaseTokenSupply) ||\n            ((type(uint256).max - mintAmount) < marketBaseTokenSupply) ||\n            ((eTWAPBaseTokenSupply < (type(uint256).max >> MAX_MINT_FACTOR_CAP)) &&\n                ((marketBaseTokenSupply + mintAmount) > (eTWAPBaseTokenSupply << MAX_MINT_FACTOR_CAP)))\n        ) {\n            revert LSErrors.E_LEX_MintCapExceeded();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures mints are capped at `min(Q96, marketBaseTokenSupply)` as originally intended, preventing oversized mints in small markets.",
